# The `checks.js` format

Every cart with `(auto)` rules has a `checks.js` beside `spec.md` (D11). It holds one check per auto rule. The playtest harness runs them; `validate_cart` makes sure they line up with the spec.

## Shape

An ES module whose default export is an object keyed by rule id. Each value is an async function that takes the test handle `t`. Every method on `t` is async, so `await` each call:

```js
// One small helper is fine; checks.js can't import anything.
const expect = (ok, why) => { if (!ok) throw new Error(why); };

/** @type {Arcade.Checks} */
export default {
  R1: async (t) => {
    const before = (await t.probe()).player.x;
    await t.input([{ frames: 10, p1: "right" }]);
    await t.step(10);
    const after = (await t.probe()).player.x;
    expect(after === before + 20, `expected x to go from ${before} to ${before + 20}, got ${after}`);
  },

  R3: async (t) => {
    // …
  },
};
```

- **Pass or fail:** a check passes if it finishes and fails if it throws. The error message becomes the reason in `playtest.json`, so make it say what was expected and what happened.
- **The handle `t`:** `await t.step(n)` runs n frames, `await t.input(script)` queues button presses, `await t.probe()` returns what the cart's `probe(g, s)` export returns, `await t.frame()` captures the current frame, `await t.sounds()` lists the sounds started, and `await t.reset(opts)` starts the cart again. The exact types are in `runtime/test-hook.d.ts`, which is the source of truth for the input script and frame formats.
- **Fresh game per check:** each check starts from a freshly loaded cart with the same seed, so checks don't depend on each other's order.
- **What a cart should expose:** give the cart's `probe(g, s)` export the state your rules talk about (positions, score, lives, the current screen). If a rule can't be checked from the probe, add what's missing to the probe rather than checking pixels.

## What validate_cart enforces

Run it with `validate-cart <cart-dir>` (or `validateCart(dir)` from this package). It checks the cart without running it; the playtest runs it. Any error means the cart doesn't publish. Warnings don't block. See D31.

**Rule coverage** (D30):

- an `(auto)` rule with no check;
- a check for a rule id the spec doesn't have;
- a check for a `(you check)` rule;
- a check that isn't a function;
- a missing `checks.js` when the spec has auto rules;
- `import`, `import()` or `require()` anywhere in `checks.js`;
- top-level code that reads files, starts processes, or runs longer than 5 seconds;
- any `spec.md` format error.

**`cart.json`:**

- it must exist and be a JSON object with `id` (lowercase letters, digits and dashes, like `sloth-snacks`), `title` and `players` (how many people the game is for, 1 to 8, the runtime's `MAX_PLAYERS`; more people than controllers means they take turns, D112);
- the fields the Studio adds when it files the cart (`version`, `maker`, `with`, `parent` as `cart-id@version` or `null`, `session_id`, `origin` as `cabinet` or `desktop`, `created` as an ISO time) must have that shape if present;
- an unknown field, or a title that differs from the spec's, is a warning.

**Cart-safe code** (D10, D13, D21), in `game.js` and `checks.js`:

- no imports of any kind in `game.js` (the starter library is `g.lib`);
- no DOM or browser APIs (`document`, `window`, `localStorage`, `requestAnimationFrame`, …);
- no network (`fetch`, `WebSocket`, `XMLHttpRequest`, workers, …);
- no `eval`, `Function` or `.constructor` tricks, and no backslash escapes in names;
- no host globals (`globalThis`, `process`, `require`, …);
- no timers (`setTimeout`, `setInterval`, …): count frames;
- no clock (`Date`, `performance`): use `g.frame`;
- no `Math.random` or `crypto`: use `g.rand`, `g.rnd` and `g.pick`;
- no `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-nocheck` or `@ts-expect-error`.

Words inside strings and comments, and property names like `s.document` or `{ fetch: 1 }`, are fine.

**Types:** `game.js` and `checks.js` are type-checked with `tsc` under `runtime/tsconfig.cart.json` (ES2023 only, no DOM, no Node, plus the `Arcade` types). `game.js` is checked as an `Arcade.Cart` and `checks.js` as `Arcade.Checks` even if you didn't annotate them, so a misspelled button name (`g.btn("jump")`), a missing `draw`, or `t.stepp()` is an error with its line. Annotating them yourself (`/** @type {Arcade.Cart<State>} */`) gets you better checks on your own state.
// @ts-check
/** @typedef {{ flow: Arcade.Lib.Flow, sc: Arcade.Lib.Score, ship: Arcade.Lib.Thing, things: Arcade.Lib.Thing[], spawn: Arcade.Lib.Timer, safe: Arcade.Lib.Timer, tick: Arcade.Lib.Timer, played: number }} State */

const SIZE = 8; // ship and comet size in pixels
const TOP = 10; // the HUD band; nothing flies there

/** @param {Arcade.G} g @param {State} s */
function newGame(g, s) {
  const { score, timers } = g.lib;
  s.sc = score.newScore(g, { lives: 3 });
  s.ship = { kind: "ship", x: 16, y: (g.H - SIZE) / 2, w: SIZE, h: SIZE };
  s.things = [];
  s.spawn = timers.timer(40, true);
  s.tick = timers.timer(60, true);
  s.safe = timers.timer(90);
  s.safe.done = true; // not safe at the start
  s.played = 0;
}

/** Comet speed after `played` frames of play (R5). */
const speedAt = (/** @type {number} */ played) => Math.min(3, 1 + 0.5 * Math.floor(played / 600));

/** @type {Arcade.Cart<State>} */
export default {
  init(g) {
    const s = /** @type {State} */ ({ flow: g.lib.flow.newFlow() });
    newGame(g, s);
    return s;
  },

  update(g, s) {
    const { flow, timers, collide, score } = g.lib;
    flow.tick(s.flow);
    if (s.flow.mode !== "play") {
      if (flow.startPressed(g, s.flow)) {
        if (s.flow.mode === "title") newGame(g, s);
        flow.go(s.flow, s.flow.mode === "title" ? "play" : "title");
      }
      return;
    }

    const sh = s.ship;
    if (g.btn("left")) sh.x -= 2;
    if (g.btn("right")) sh.x += 2;
    if (g.btn("up")) sh.y -= 2;
    if (g.btn("down")) sh.y += 2;
    sh.x = Math.max(0, Math.min(g.W - SIZE, sh.x));
    sh.y = Math.max(TOP, Math.min(g.H - SIZE, sh.y));

    s.played += 1;
    if (timers.tick(s.tick)) score.addPoints(s.sc, 1);
    if (timers.tick(s.spawn)) s.things.push({ kind: "comet", x: g.W, y: TOP + g.rnd(g.H - TOP - SIZE), w: SIZE, h: SIZE });
    const v = speedAt(s.played);
    for (const c of s.things) if (c.kind === "comet") c.x -= v;

    timers.tick(s.safe);
    const hits = collide.resolve([sh, ...s.things], [{ a: "comet", b: "ship", kill: s.safe.done ? "a" : "none" }]);
    if (hits.length && s.safe.done) {
      g.sfx("hit");
      timers.restart(s.safe);
      if (score.loseLife(s.sc) === 0) {
        score.commit(g, s.sc);
        g.sfx("lose");
        flow.go(s.flow, "over");
      }
    }
    s.things = collide.alive(s.things).filter((c) => c.x > -SIZE);
  },

  draw(g, s) {
    const c = g.colors;
    g.cls(c.night);
    for (let i = 0; i < 24; i++) g.pset((((i * 53 - (g.frame >> 1)) % g.W) + g.W) % g.W, (i * 37) % g.H, c.slate);
    const mid = g.W / 2;
    if (s.flow.mode === "title") {
      g.text("COMET DODGE", mid, g.H / 3, c.sky, { scale: 2, align: "center" });
      if (g.frame % 60 < 30) g.text("PRESS START", mid, g.H / 2, c.white, { align: "center" });
      g.text(`BEST ${s.sc.best}`, mid, g.H / 2 + 20, c.gold, { align: "center" });
      return;
    }
    for (const t of s.things) g.circfill(t.x + 4, t.y + 4, 4, c.orange);
    const blink = !s.safe.done && g.frame % 8 < 4;
    if (!blink) g.rectfill(s.ship.x, s.ship.y, SIZE, SIZE, c.sky);
    g.text(`SCORE ${s.sc.score}  LIVES ${s.sc.lives}  BEST ${s.sc.best}`, 2, 1, c.white);
    if (s.flow.mode === "over") g.text("GAME OVER", mid, g.H / 2 - 8, c.red, { scale: 2, align: "center" });
  },

  probe(g, s) {
    const comets = s.things.map((t) => ({ x: t.x, y: t.y }));
    return { W: g.W, H: g.H, mode: s.flow.mode, ship: { x: s.ship.x, y: s.ship.y }, comets, score: s.sc.score, lives: s.sc.lives, safe: !s.safe.done, speed: speedAt(s.played), played: s.played };
  },
};
// @ts-check
// Helpers live here: checks.js imports nothing.
const expect = (ok, why) => {
  if (!ok) throw new Error(why);
};

/** From the title, press START and land in play. START is ignored for the title's first 30 frames. */
async function start(t) {
  await t.step(31);
  await t.input([{ frames: 1, p1: "start" }]);
  await t.step(2);
  expect((await t.probe()).mode === "play", "pressing START on the title should start the game");
}

/** Steer the ship up or down, `frames` frames at a time, until `done(p)` or `max` frames. away=true dodges, false chases. */
async function fly(t, away, max, done) {
  for (let n = 0; n < max; n += 2) {
    const p = await t.probe();
    if (done(p)) return p;
    const ahead = p.comets.filter((c) => c.x > p.ship.x - 8 && c.x < p.ship.x + 48);
    let dir = "";
    if (away) {
      const near = ahead.find((c) => Math.abs(c.y - p.ship.y) < 14);
      if (near) dir = near.y > p.ship.y ? (p.ship.y > 20 ? "up" : "down") : p.ship.y < p.H - 20 ? "down" : "up";
    } else if (ahead.length) {
      dir = ahead[0].y > p.ship.y ? "down" : ahead[0].y < p.ship.y ? "up" : "";
    }
    await t.input([{ frames: 2, p1: dir }]);
    await t.step(2);
  }
  return t.probe();
}

/** @type {Arcade.Checks} */
export default {
  async R1(t) {
    await start(t);
    const a = await t.probe();
    await t.input([{ frames: 10, p1: "down" }]);
    await t.step(10);
    const b = await t.probe();
    expect(b.ship.y === a.ship.y + 20, `holding down for 10 frames should move the ship from y=${a.ship.y} to ${a.ship.y + 20}, got ${b.ship.y}`);
    await t.input([{ frames: 10, p1: "right" }]);
    await t.step(10);
    const c = await t.probe();
    expect(c.ship.x === b.ship.x + 20, `holding right for 10 frames should move the ship from x=${b.ship.x} to ${b.ship.x + 20}, got ${c.ship.x}`);
    await t.input([{ frames: 400, p1: "right down" }]);
    await t.step(400);
    const d = await t.probe();
    expect(d.ship.x === d.W - 8 && d.ship.y === d.H - 8, `the ship should stop in the bottom-right corner (${d.W - 8}, ${d.H - 8}), but it's at (${d.ship.x}, ${d.ship.y})`);
  },

  async R2(t) {
    await start(t);
    const p = await fly(t, true, 300, (p) => p.played >= 300);
    expect(p.lives === 3, "the dodge helper got hit, so the score can't be read cleanly");
    expect(p.score === Math.floor(p.played / 60), `after ${p.played} frames of play the score should be ${Math.floor(p.played / 60)}, got ${p.score}`);
  },

  async R3(t) {
    await start(t);
    const hit = await fly(t, false, 1200, (p) => p.lives < 3);
    expect(hit.lives === 2, `chasing comets should cost exactly one life on the first touch, lives are ${hit.lives}`);
    expect(hit.safe, "right after a hit the ship should be safe");
    const after = await fly(t, false, 84, () => false);
    expect(after.lives === 2, `comets shouldn't hurt the ship in the 90 frames after a hit, but lives went to ${after.lives}`);
    const later = await fly(t, false, 1200, (p) => p.lives < 2);
    expect(later.lives === 1, `once the safe time is over a comet should take another life, lives are ${later.lives}`);
  },

  async R4(t) {
    await start(t);
    const p = await fly(t, false, 4000, (p) => p.lives === 0);
    expect(p.lives === 0, "couldn't lose every life by chasing comets");
    await t.step(30);
    const q = await t.probe();
    expect(q.mode === "over", `at 0 lives the game-over screen should show, but the mode is "${q.mode}"`);
  },

  async R5(t) {
    await start(t);
    // Measure real comet movement over one frame, at points along the curve.
    for (const at of [60, 610, 1210, 2410, 3010]) {
      const p = await fly(t, true, 4000, (p) => (p.played >= at && p.comets.length > 0) || p.mode !== "play");
      expect(p.mode === "play", `the dodge helper lost every life before frame ${at} of play`);
      await t.step(1);
      const q = await t.probe();
      const c = q.comets.find((c) => c.y === p.comets[0].y);
      const want = Math.min(3, 1 + 0.5 * Math.floor(q.played / 600));
      expect(c && p.comets[0].x - c.x === want, `after ${q.played} frames of play comets should move ${want} pixels per frame, moved ${c ? p.comets[0].x - c.x : "?"}`);
    }
  },
};
# Comet Dodge

maker: C · with: — · parent: — · session: —

## Pitch

You fly a little ship. Comets zoom in from the right. Don't get hit, and stay alive as long as you can.

## Rules

- R1 (auto): Holding a direction moves the ship 2 pixels per frame that way. It stops at the edges of the screen.
- R2 (auto): While playing, the score goes up by 1 every 60 frames.
- R3 (auto): Touching a comet takes away one life and removes that comet. For the next 90 frames
  comets can't hurt the ship.
- R4 (auto): At 0 lives the game-over screen shows within 30 frames.
- R5 (auto): Comets start at 1 pixel per frame and get 0.5 faster every 600 frames of play, up to 3.
- R6 (you check): The ship blinks while comets can't hurt it.

## Controls

- Player 1: stick moves the ship.
- START or A: start from the title screen, and go back to it after game over.
- ★ is the cabinet's button; games never use it.

## Look & sound

Dark night sky with drifting star dots. A sky-blue ship, orange comets. A "hit" sound when a comet gets you, a "lose" sound at game over.

## Assumptions

- A1: Three lives, because the request didn't say.
- A2: A new comet every 40 frames, at a random height.

## Changelog

- v1: First version, an example cart for the context pack.
{ "id": "comet-dodge", "title": "Comet Dodge", "players": 1 }
// House runtime test hook (D10, D11, D21): the `t` handle every check gets.
//
// A cart's checks.js is an ES module whose default export maps each auto rule
// id in spec.md to an async check:
//
//   // @ts-check
//   /** @type {Arcade.Checks} */
//   export default {
//     async R1(t) {
//       await t.input([{ frames: 30, p1: "right" }]);
//       await t.step(30);
//       const p = await t.probe();
//       if (!(p.x > 128)) throw new Error(`holding right for 30 frames should move the cat right, x=${p.x}`);
//     },
//   };
//
// A check passes by returning and fails by throwing; the error message is the
// reason shown in playtest.json, so write it for a person. Checks import
// nothing (no node:assert): they must run the same in Node and in a browser.
//
// Every check starts from a fresh cart: seed 1, nothing saved, frame 0, one
// controller (g.players === 1, as on the cabinet), init already run. Time
// only moves when the check calls step(): the clock is frames, not wall time,
// so a report reproduces on any machine.
//
// The same handle is implemented three ways, all with this exact shape:
//   - headless in Node (runtime/headless.js): no browser, no pixels on screen;
//   - inside the cart frame (runtime/frame.js), driven by the host page;
//   - on the host page as `window.arcade.test` (runtime/host.js), which
//     forwards to the frame; this is what Playwright drives.

declare namespace Arcade {
  /**
   * Buttons held for one input step: an array of buttons, or a
   * space-separated string such as "right a". "" or [] holds nothing.
   */
  type Held = readonly Button[] | string;

  /**
   * Hold these buttons for `frames` frames. Players left out hold nothing.
   * A player beyond the run's controller count (1 unless `reset({ players })`
   * says otherwise, D110) has no controller, so what it holds is ignored.
   */
  interface InputStep {
    /** How many frames this step lasts, >= 1. */
    frames: number;
    p1?: Held;
    p2?: Held;
    p3?: Held;
    p4?: Held;
    p5?: Held;
    p6?: Held;
    p7?: Held;
    p8?: Held;
  }

  /** A queue of input steps, played in order, one frame per stepped frame. */
  type InputScript = readonly InputStep[];

  /** A captured frame: what the screen shows after the last draw. */
  interface Frame {
    /** g.frame when this was drawn. */
    frame: number;
    width: 256;
    height: 192;
    /** Palette indices (0..31), row-major: pixel (x, y) is pixels[y * width + x]. */
    pixels: ArrayLike<number>;
    /** Hash of the pixels (hex). Same seed + same input => same hash. */
    hash: string;
  }

  interface ResetOptions {
    /** RNG seed for the new run. Default 1. */
    seed?: number;
    /** Keep what the cart saved with g.save() (e.g. to test a high score that survives a restart). Default false. */
    keepSave?: boolean;
    /**
     * Controllers for the new run, 1..8 (g.players). Default: the run's
     * starting count, which is 1 (the cabinet) unless the harness says
     * otherwise. Use `{ players: 2 }` to check a side-by-side game.
     */
    players?: number;
  }

  /** A sound the cart started, for checks like "a sound plays when a line clears". */
  interface SoundEvent {
    frame: number;
    /** "tone" for g.tone, otherwise the g.sfx name. */
    kind: "tone" | Sfx;
    voice: number;
  }

  /** A dev warning: something the cart did that works but is probably a mistake. */
  interface Warning {
    frame: number;
    message: string;
  }

  interface TestHandle {
    /**
     * Advance n frames (default 1): each frame runs update, consuming one
     * frame of the input queue (nothing held once it is empty), and the last
     * one runs draw. Rejects with the cart's error if update or draw throws.
     */
    step(n?: number): Promise<void>;
    /** Append steps to the input queue. Nothing happens until step(). */
    input(script: InputScript): Promise<void>;
    /** The cart's `probe(g, s)` result right now (JSON-cloned), or null if it has none. */
    probe(): Promise<any>;
    /** Capture the current screen. */
    frame(): Promise<Frame>;
    /** Sounds started since the last reset, oldest first. */
    sounds(): Promise<SoundEvent[]>;
    /**
     * Dev warnings since the last reset, each kind once: today, a color outside
     * 0..31 (it still draws, wrapped into the palette). For the palette check.
     * Optional so older handle wrappers stay valid; every runtime handle has it.
     */
    warnings?(): Promise<Warning[]>;
    /** Restart the cart from init with a fresh RNG and an empty input queue. */
    reset(opts?: ResetOptions): Promise<void>;
  }

  /** A cart's checks.js default export: one check per auto rule id ("R1", "R3"...). */
  type Checks = Record<string, (t: TestHandle) => Promise<void> | void>;
}

// House runtime cart API (D3, D10, D11, D21).
//
// Global declarations, so a cart can be type-checked from anywhere with no
// import paths: put this file (and test-hook.d.ts for checks.js) in the tsc
// file list and annotate the cart with JSDoc:
//
//   // @ts-check
//   /** @typedef {{ x: number, score: number }} State */
//   /** @type {Arcade.Cart<State>} */
//   export default {
//     init(g) { return { x: g.W / 2, score: 0 }; },
//     update(g, s) { if (g.btn("right")) s.x += 1; },
//     draw(g, s) { g.cls(0); g.text("HI", s.x, 120, g.colors.white); },
//     probe(g, s) { return { x: s.x, score: s.score }; },
//   };
//
// Rules a cart lives by: the screen is 256x192 with 32 fixed colors; input is
// the cabinet's buttons only (g.players controllers, 1 on the cabinet); no DOM, no network, no timers, no Math.random
// (use g.rand); persistence only through g.save()/g.load().

declare namespace Arcade {
  /** Screen width in pixels. */
  type Width = 256;
  /** Screen height in pixels. */
  type Height = 192;

  /** A palette index, 0..31. See `G.colors` for names. */
  type Color = number;

  /** The cabinet's buttons per player. ★ (the Picade's ESC button, Escape on the dev keyboard) leaves the game and goes back to the shelf (D88); it belongs to the cabinet and never reaches a cart. */
  type Button = "up" | "down" | "left" | "right" | "a" | "b" | "x" | "y" | "start";

  /**
   * Player number, 1..8: player p uses controller p. The console has
   * `g.players` controllers (1 on the cabinet, D110); a player beyond that
   * has no controller, so its buttons always read as not held (D112). For a
   * game for more people than controllers, take turns with `g.lib.turns`.
   */
  type Player = 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8;

  /** The 32 palette names, mapped to their index. */
  interface Colors {
    black: 0; night: 1; slate: 2; gray: 3; silver: 4; white: 5;
    maroon: 6; red: 7; coral: 8; pink: 9;
    brown: 10; orange: 11; tan: 12; peach: 13;
    gold: 14; yellow: 15;
    forest: 16; green: 17; lime: 18; mint: 19;
    teal: 20; aqua: 21;
    navy: 22; blue: 23; sky: 24; ice: 25;
    indigo: 26; purple: 27; lilac: 28;
    plum: 29; magenta: 30; rose: 31;
  }

  /** A palette-indexed sprite made by `g.sprite()`. */
  interface Sprite {
    readonly w: number;
    readonly h: number;
    /** w*h palette indices, row-major; -1 is transparent. */
    readonly data: Int8Array;
  }

  interface SprOptions {
    flipX?: boolean;
    flipY?: boolean;
    /** Integer scale, 1..8. Default 1. */
    scale?: number;
    /** Draw every opaque pixel in this one color (flashes, shadows). */
    color?: Color;
  }

  interface TextOptions {
    /** Integer scale, 1..8. Default 1: glyphs are 5x7 (plus a descender row), 6 pixels apart, lines 9 apart. */
    scale?: number;
    /** Where `x` sits relative to the text. Default "left". */
    align?: "left" | "center" | "right";
  }

  /** Chip-synth waveforms. */
  type Wave = "square" | "pulse" | "triangle" | "saw" | "noise";

  /** One note on the 4-voice chip synth. */
  interface Tone {
    /** Pitch as a note name ("C4", "F#5", "Bb3") or a frequency in Hz. Default "A4". */
    note?: string | number;
    /** Default "square". */
    wave?: Wave;
    /** Length in frames (60 per second). Default 8. */
    frames?: number;
    /** Volume 0..1. Default 0.5. */
    vol?: number;
    /** Pitch change over the note, in semitones (e.g. 12 slides up an octave). Default 0. */
    slide?: number;
    /** Voice 0..3. A new tone on a busy voice replaces it. Default: the next free voice, else the oldest. */
    voice?: number;
  }

  /** Built-in sound effects. */
  type Sfx = "blip" | "select" | "jump" | "coin" | "pop" | "hit" | "explode" | "powerup" | "lose" | "win";

  interface SfxOptions {
    /** Shift the effect up or down, in semitones. Default 0. */
    pitch?: number;
    /** Volume 0..1. Default 0.5. */
    vol?: number;
    voice?: number;
  }

  /** The console handle passed to every cart function. */
  interface G {
    readonly W: Width;
    readonly H: Height;
    /** Frames since `init` (0 during init, 1 in the first update). The clock is a fixed 60 Hz. */
    readonly frame: number;
    /** Palette names to indices, e.g. `g.colors.red`. */
    readonly colors: Readonly<Colors>;
    /** The palette as "#rrggbb" strings, index 0..31. */
    readonly palette: readonly string[];
    /** The starter library (D50): pure helpers, shared by every cart. Carts reach it here instead of importing it. */
    readonly lib: typeof import("./lib/index.js");
    /**
